media: h713-hdmirx: S_EDID takes one 256-byte EDID as well, and serves it

The receiver's EDID store is two 256-byte halves; a source is served one of
them, and which one is the ARISC firmware's per-port block mask, board policy
on the arisc node (0091c) and 0 on both boards. The HY310's second half is the
vendor's HDMI 2.0 set: it adds 1080p100/120, which the display firmware's own
mode table carries as enabled records 13 and 15, plus 4K50/60, which that table
has disabled (umbau/work/FB-EDID/tables/edid-vs-firmware.csv). Reaching the two
100/120 Hz modes therefore needs the second half in front of the source.

The mask is not the way there at run time. Measured twice (dev20 with the
handler race, dev21 without it): SetEDIDVersion alone publishes nothing, the
byte is read where fragment 7 publishes, and the sequence that ends in a
publish - arisc_hdmi_edid_init() - begins with ResetEDIDModule, which zeroes
the byte (AP3l P3). The one run that showed block 1 at the PC (dev20) went
through the racy 0x0f, so whether the guarded SetEDIDVersion behind fragment 7
republishes port 0 is open. And the release kernel has no debugfs, so the
bench command is not a switch a service can use.

So S_EDID gets a second shape. Four blocks stay what they were, the whole
store. Two blocks are one EDID: it becomes the first half, the one every port
is served under mask 0, and the second half keeps what it held - or a copy,
when nothing was uploaded yet. Every firmware-side republish (HostHDMIMAP,
ResetEDIDModule, fragment 7; AP3l P2-P4) serves the store with the mask it
has, so the served half stays whatever the firmware does behind the driver.
G_EDID is unchanged and returns the store as it is now.

h713-tv uses it for "ctl edid block0|block1" and the tv.conf key "edid": the
named half of /lib/firmware/hy310-edid.bin as one 256-byte S_EDID inside its
replug cycle. Nothing changes for a client that sends four blocks, and the
probe's own sequence is untouched.

Not built here (FB-EDID wrote no kernel in this package); applies to the tree
0136y leaves with patch -p1 --dry-run.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -4198,12 +4198,28 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_s_edid(struct file *file, void *priv,
 		return arisc_hdmi_hpd(H713_HDMI_PORT, ARISC_HDMI_HPD_DOWN);
 	}
 
-	if (edid->blocks != ARISC_HDMI_EDID_SIZE / 128) {
+	if (edid->blocks != ARISC_HDMI_EDID_SIZE / 128 &&
+	    edid->blocks != ARISC_HDMI_EDID_BLOCK / 128) {
 		edid->blocks = ARISC_HDMI_EDID_SIZE / 128;
 		return -E2BIG;
 	}
 
-	memcpy(rx->edid, edid->edid, ARISC_HDMI_EDID_SIZE);
+	/*
+	 * Two blocks are one 256-byte EDID: it becomes the half the port is
+	 * served (the first, under the board's mask 0), the other half keeps
+	 * what it held, or a copy when nothing was uploaded yet. That is how
+	 * h713-tv publishes the second half of hy310-edid.bin without touching
+	 * the ARISC's per-port mask (FB-EDID): every firmware-side republish
+	 * serves the store with the mask it has, so the served half stays.
+	 */
+	if (edid->blocks == ARISC_HDMI_EDID_BLOCK / 128) {
+		if (!rx->edid_valid)
+			memcpy(rx->edid + ARISC_HDMI_EDID_BLOCK, edid->edid,
+			       ARISC_HDMI_EDID_BLOCK);
+		memcpy(rx->edid, edid->edid, ARISC_HDMI_EDID_BLOCK);
+	} else {
+		memcpy(rx->edid, edid->edid, ARISC_HDMI_EDID_SIZE);
+	}
 
 	ret = arisc_hdmi_edid_init(H713_HDMI_PORT, rx->edid,
 				   ARISC_HDMI_EDID_SIZE);
